          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-5f3ea2b0816eebde\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.88,\"analysis\":\"The written clause primarily avows Widdowson’s wholehearted love as a fact Monica already knows. Its courtship setting gives it slight commitment-invoking force, but it does not itself clearly undertake a new obligation. The claim that affectionate words come to his lips and pen “irresistibly” provides positive, though not decisive, evidence that the language also functions as affect-driven expression rather than solely as an intense avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The metapragmatic claim of irresistible expression supports E, but it may apply more directly to the surrounding endearments than to the exact declarative clause. P is also limited because romantic courtship and solicitation do not by themselves constitute an undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Widdowson writes the love avowal in a private letter to Monica, which Monica subsequently reads.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Widdowson uses the avowal amid anxious requests for contact and despite Monica’s instruction not to use affectionate language. It reassures and courts her while also pressing against a stated boundary. Those interpersonal pressures shape the letter’s effect but do not displace the core truth-presenting avowal. The surrounding profusion of endearments and his description of them as irresistible add some independently expressive force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“You know so well that I love you with all my heart and soul”\",\"supports\":\"Strong T: the writer explicitly presents his wholehearted loving state as true and already known to Monica.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“these words of affection; they come to my lips and to my pen irresistibly”\",\"supports\":\"Moderate E: the writer characterizes his affectionate language as an involuntary or affect-driven verbal production.\",\"confidence\":0.86},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“It’s no use telling me that I must not use these words of affection”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs in tension with Monica’s stated boundary, contributing pressure and persistence as contextual effects rather than a distinct core-force category.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The letter asks Monica to write and arrange another appointment, while Widdowson says he is barely keeping his promise not to come and ask about her.\",\"supports\":\"The courtship context weakly supports relationally invoking P, though the exact love clause contains no clear new promise or undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.91},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The letter continues for four close pages and includes “My darling” and further affectionate addresses.\",\"supports\":\"Supports the interpretation of sustained emotional and courtship expression, while intensity and repetition alone do not establish E or P.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the dominant avowal, with modest P and E representing respectively its courtship-related relational invocation and its explicitly presented affect-driven production. No important aspect of the core force requires O; boundary pressure and possible manipulation belong to contextual interpretation.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
